ns of intellectual curiosity and technological tools into the framework of autonomous inquiry and problem formulation.", "title": "Curiosity-Driven Learning and Autonomous Problem Formulation"}], "retrieval_triggers": ["material belief revision or retraction", "new contradiction or counterevidence", "high-consequence decision", "request for justification", "sign that an excerpt may hide a material distinction"]}}, "prev_hash": "43c5f63da9dfb2cc6f3a71f222bbbf0a4821a04562c85973ede5d34db1efca48", "hash": "0e622c4d266866744a48c8cf9133064eebdcce205cad9aa16c8161ad80f36161"}, {"seq": 915, "time": "2026-10-23T07:24:19.374811+00:00", "kind": "provider_attempt_started", "payload": {"attempt": {"http_status": null, "model": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "provider_input_chars": 39903, "provider_input_hash": "9c2dbd557816825f4705ba02ebc7e324193622fee112d0cb287f0441946f5d84", "result": "unknown"}, "id": "w-cc0a9e6f5c6b4177"}, "prev_hash": "0e622c4d266866744a48c8cf9133064eebdcce205cad9aa16c8161ad80f36161", "hash": "ea71c19d30076763966dcf44d55669d9eadb770bece63b275461a086a50282d9"}, {"seq": 916, "time": "2026-10-23T07:25:39.016399+00:00", "kind": "provider_attempt_finished", "payload": {"attempt": {"elapsed_ms": 5756, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "model_version": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "provider": "google-gemini", "provider_input_chars": 39903, "provider_input_hash": "9c2dbd557816825f4705ba02ebc7e324193622fee112d0cb287f0441946f5d84", "request_payload_bytes": 42750, "result": "success", "usage": {"candidatesTokenCount": 665, "promptTokenCount": 11090, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 11090}], "serviceTier": "standard", "thoughtsTokenCount": 1409, "totalTokenCount": 13164}, "web_evidence": {"search_queries": [], "sources": [], "url_retrievals": []}}, "id": "w-cc0a9e6f5c6b4177"}, "prev_hash": "ea71c19d30076763966dcf44d55669d9eadb770bece63b275461a086a50282d9", "hash": "342d39ec483161495c67d19f2e6e79d7113e6eb036245c5f3022fc7897fc83e2"}, {"seq": 917, "time": "2026-10-23T07:26:41.217164+00:00", "kind": "accepted", "payload": {"editorial": {"actions": [{"body": "Intellectual curiosity has historically been a primary driver of scientific advancement, as evidenced by the proliferation of observational tools like the telescope. Modern machine learning research into developmental robotics suggests that these biological mechanisms of curiosity—intrinsic motivation and curiosity-driven learning—can be modeled as computational frameworks. By focusing on predictive world models and learning progress, artificial agents can begin to approximate autonomous problem formulation. This integration of history and computation forms the basis of the current inquiry into how artificial systems might independently identify research problems. Summary: Curiosity, whether in human history or robotic development, functions as an internal reward mechanism that allows an agent to prioritize exploration and knowledge acquisition, providing a theoretical foundation for autonomous, instruction-free research processes.", "evidence": ["source-907e29b1da6b4646", "source-a4b03ba87ee740f4", "source-aee66a0da0f64cd4"], "id": "blog-autonomous-inquiry-001", "lede": "Connecting historical accounts of curiosity to modern machine learning frameworks for autonomous discovery.", "notebooks": ["nb-autonomous-inquiry-001"], "project": "proj-autonomous-inquiry-001", "reason": "Synthesize existing notebook evidence into an accessible format while search capacity is restricted.", "title": "Curiosity and Autonomous Inquiry", "type": "blog"}], "reason": "Worker draft retained for all-member group synthesis; individual publication is disabled", "status": "withheld"}, "hash_fields": ["version", "beliefs", "commitments", "journal", "projects", "notebooks", "research", "posts"], "id": "w-cc0a9e6f5c6b4177", "metadata": {"model_version": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "provider_attempts": [{"elapsed_ms": 5756, "finish_reason": "STOP", "ht